"""
coordinator-precommit-settings-tracking-check — pre-commit gate for the
~/.claude meta-repo: refuse a commit that (re-)stages any of the tracked
machine-local settings sidecars/markers named in `_TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS`
below, regardless of what those files contain.

Why content-blind, unlike the sibling foreign-platform check: those three
paths were untracked and `.gitignore`d (`settings.json` in `0966c14`;
`.settings-last-good.json` / `.coordinator-settings-hooks.hash` in the
2026-07-28 settings-tracking-leak cleanup) precisely BECAUSE they are
machine-local and carry machine-absolute paths — the untrack is the fix, and
`.gitignore` alone does not hold: `git add -f` bypasses it outright, and this
repo's history is full of blind `safety-commit`/`safety` sweeps that don't
distinguish tracked-on-purpose from tracked-by-accident (`ed0b972`, "safety",
is the commit that landed the 2026-07-28 corruption). A losing machine that
re-tracks `.settings-last-good.json` specifically doesn't just inherit a
foreign `settings.json` on its next pull — the clobber-guard's own
AUTO-RESTORE path (`coordinator_core.ops.session.guard_settings_integrity`)
will then install that foreign snapshot BACK OVER a healthy local
`settings.json` the next time this guard's clobber lens fires, so a tracked
snapshot is a strictly worse leak vector than a tracked `settings.json`
itself. `.coordinator-settings-hooks.hash` is a digest of that same
machine-local content and leaks the same shape of information.

This is a STAGING-time gate, not a content-inspection one: it fires on the
mere presence of any of the three basenames in the staged file list, with no
attempt to parse or shape-check their content — unlike
`coordinator-precommit-foreign-platform-check`, which inspects staged
`settings.json` content for foreign-platform-shaped paths. That check still
matters for any residual settings.json history/edge case; this one is the
structural backstop that makes "was it ever staged at all" the question,
because content-shape detection cannot see a `git add -f` coming.

Two-lens design (2026-08-11, DoE-claude cross-repo memo
`2026-08-11-doe-claude-em-two-gaps-that-let-machine-local-files-stay-tracked.md`):
`_TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS` alone is a root-level EXACT-basename match — it ran
clean while a full `~/.claude` `settings.json` snapshot sat tracked under
`settings.json.bak-2026-08-07-dead-hooks`, and while byte-identical
`.claude.settings.json` / `.claude.settings.local.json` / plugin-manifest
snapshots sat tracked inside a `_prag-fix-backup-*/` directory — the same
leak, one basename-shift or one directory-nest away from the matcher's
blind spot. Rather than widen `_TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS` (which would break its
content-blind exact-match contract — every entry there names a file this repo
has protected since a specific incident, not a naming pattern), a SECOND lens
runs alongside it: `_TRACKED_SIDECAR_GLOBS`, a set of `fnmatch` basename
patterns checked against every staged path's basename at ANY depth, catching
backup/snapshot NAMING SHAPES (`settings.json.bak*`, dotted `.claude.settings*`
copies, `plugins.<name>.json` manifest snapshots) regardless of which
directory they were swept into. Both lenses report into the same offender
list and the same exit-1 block; each offender line is tagged with which lens
fired (`exact` vs `glob`) so the operator knows this is still an untrack, not
a `.gitignore` edit — a `.gitignore` addition does not retroactively untrack
anything already committed, and does not stop `git add -f`.

Scope: only fires inside the meta-repo (git root == CLAUDE_HOME/.claude), same
discriminator as the sibling `coordinator-precommit-foreign-platform-check`
and `coordinator-precommit-exec-bit-check`. Elsewhere it exits 0 immediately.

DETECT-ONLY, never auto-fixes: unstaging a file the operator explicitly
`git add -f`'d is a policy call (did they mean it?), not something this gate
should silently override. A human/PM call picks the remedy.

Exit contract:
  0 — clean (nothing in `_TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS` staged and no staged
      basename matches `_TRACKED_SIDECAR_GLOBS`), or not the meta-repo, or
      override set.
  1 — one or more offenders from either lens is staged. Blocks the commit;
      loud stderr banner naming each offending path (tagged `[exact match]`
      or `[glob match]`) + the `git rm --cached` remedy + override hint.

Spec backlink: docs/plans (DoE-claude) state/subagent-share/
  f2de80af-e8ff-4401-9970-df82761d913e/coordinatorexecutor-fc9a7a0a.md
Sibling: coordinator-precommit-foreign-platform-check,
  coordinator-precommit-exec-bit-check (same meta-repo-scoping shape).

Invocation (2026-07-28): carries no shebang and no exec bit. It is invoked
ONLY via an explicit interpreter (`coordinator_core.ops.
install_meta_repo_precommit_hook`'s generated hook body runs
`"$_py" "$_gate_script"`, never bare-name/shebang-exec) — POSIX-only
execution assumptions are a portability defect class per the 2026-07-28 PM
ruling (Windows is the P0 primary platform), enforced by
`coordinator_core.ops.check_posix_exec_assumptions`.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import fnmatch
import os
import subprocess
import sys

_BIN_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
_LIB_DIR = os.path.join(_BIN_DIR, "lib")
if _LIB_DIR not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, _LIB_DIR)

_OVERRIDE_ENV = "COORDINATOR_OVERRIDE_PRECOMMIT_SETTINGS_TRACKING"


def _override_doc_pointer() -> str:
    """SSOT doc-pointer display string, imported lazily -- this script's
    early-exit legs (override-already-set skip, below) fire before any other
    sys.path setup this file does, so this resolves its own path
    independent of that ordering. Never hand-copy the "claude-klabauter
    <path>" string here (B6/B8, see docs/wiki/guard-messaging.md §
    Register)."""
    root = os.path.abspath(os.path.join(_BIN_DIR, "..", ".."))
    if root not in sys.path:
        sys.path.insert(0, root)
    from coordinator_core.bash_guards._helpers import OVERRIDE_KEYS_DOC_DISPLAY

    return OVERRIDE_KEYS_DOC_DISPLAY

# Root-level basenames only (mirrors the .gitignore anchoring: leading `/`
# anchors to repo root in gitignore syntax; `git diff --name-only` paths are
# already repo-root-relative with no leading slash, so the bare basename is
# the correct staged-path form to match against here).
#
# 2026-07-28 addition: the two `.coordinator-hooks-*` kill-switch markers and
# the self-probe sentinel are added here for the SAME reason as the original
# three — all three are per-machine, gitignored, and their entire safety
# property depends on staying that way. `~/.claude` is the repo that was
# ACTUALLY corrupted in the 2026-07-28 incident, is not onboarded via
# repo-setup, and so is structurally excluded from every OTHER provisioning
# path that covers a normal repo — this pre-commit gate chain is the only
# mechanism that reaches it at all. A tracked `.coordinator-hooks-enabled`
# (the positive consent marker, `coordinator_core.install.gen_settings_hooks`)
# is the exact transmission vector the polarity inversion was built to
# resist: syncing it to a peer would silently opt that peer's machine INTO
# hook generation regardless of its own local state. A tracked
# `.coordinator-hooks-disabled` or `.coordinator-content-root-last-seen`
# (the self-probe sentinel, `coordinator_core.ops.session.
# guard_hook_generation_self_probe`) leaks one machine's kill-switch/content-
# root state onto another's, which is either a false-negative (peer's real
# kill-switch state gets silently overwritten) or a confusing false report
# (sentinel content that never reflected this machine at all).
_TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS = (
    "settings.json",
    ".settings-last-good.json",
    ".coordinator-settings-hooks.hash",
    ".coordinator-hooks-disabled",
    ".coordinator-hooks-enabled",
    ".coordinator-content-root-last-seen",
)

# Second lens (2026-08-11): basename-shape globs, checked against every
# staged path's basename at ANY depth — not just repo root. These catch
# settings/plugin-manifest BACKUP SNAPSHOTS whose basename or containing
# directory shifted just enough to slip past the exact-match list above,
# evidenced verbatim by the 2026-08-11 `~/.claude` leak (see module
# docstring "Two-lens design"): `settings.json.bak-2026-08-07-dead-hooks` at
# root, and `.claude.settings.json` / `.claude.settings.local.json` /
# `plugins.installed_plugins.json` / `plugins.known_marketplaces.json` nested
# under `_prag-fix-backup-20260720T144452Z/`. Deliberately basename-only
# (`fnmatch` against `os.path.basename(...)`, not the full path) so a rename
# of the containing backup directory can't defeat it the way a root-anchored
# `_TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS` entry can be defeated by nesting.
_TRACKED_SIDECAR_GLOBS = (
    "settings.json.bak*",
    "*.claude.settings*.json",
    "plugins.*.json",
)


def _git_out(args: list, cwd: str = None) -> str:
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["git", *args],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=30,
            cwd=cwd,
        )
    except Exception:
        return ""
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return ""
    return out.stdout or ""


def _resolve_meta_repo() -> str:
    """Meta-repo root via the canonical CLAUDE_HOME-aware claude-home resolver
    (same seam as the sibling precommit checks)."""
    claude_home_lib = os.path.join(_BIN_DIR, "..", "lib", "claude-home")
    try:
        if claude_home_lib not in sys.path:
            sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath(claude_home_lib))
        import _claude_home  # noqa: E402

        return str(_claude_home.claude_home_dir())
    except Exception:
        base = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~")
        return os.path.join(base, ".claude")


def _canon(path: str) -> str:
    try:
        return os.path.realpath(path)
    except OSError:
        return path


def main() -> int:
    repo_root = _git_out(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"]).strip()
    if not repo_root:
        return 0

    meta_repo = _resolve_meta_repo()
    if not meta_repo:
        return 0

    if _canon(repo_root) != _canon(meta_repo):
        return 0

    if os.environ.get(_OVERRIDE_ENV) == "1":
        print(
            "pre-commit: settings-tracking check skipped (override set).",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 0

    staged_files = [
        ln
        for ln in _git_out(
            ["-C", meta_repo, "diff", "--cached", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=d"]
        ).splitlines()
        if ln
    ]

    exact_offenders = [p for p in _TRACKED_SIDECAR_PATHS if p in staged_files]
    glob_offenders = [
        p
        for p in staged_files
        if p not in exact_offenders
        and any(fnmatch.fnmatch(os.path.basename(p), pat) for pat in _TRACKED_SIDECAR_GLOBS)
    ]
    offenders = [(p, "exact") for p in exact_offenders] + [(p, "glob") for p in glob_offenders]
    if not offenders:
        return 0

    top = "=" * 70
    print(top, file=sys.stderr)
    print(
        "pre-commit: BLOCKED — this commit re-tracks a machine-local settings "
        "sidecar in the ~/.claude meta-repo:",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    for p, lens in offenders:
        print(f"    {p}  [{lens} match]", file=sys.stderr)
    print("", file=sys.stderr)
    print(
        "  Why this is blocked: these files carry machine-absolute paths and "
        "are gitignored on purpose (settings.json in 0966c14; its sidecars in "
        "the 2026-07-28 cleanup). `.settings-last-good.json` specifically is "
        "the snapshot the clobber-guard AUTO-RESTORES FROM — a tracked copy "
        "doesn't just leak a foreign settings.json to the next machine that "
        "pulls it, that machine's own repair mechanism then installs the "
        "foreign snapshot over its own healthy settings.json.",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    print("", file=sys.stderr)
    print("  Remedy: unstage, then commit without them:", file=sys.stderr)
    for p, _lens in offenders:
        print(f'    git rm --cached "{p}"', file=sys.stderr)
    print("", file=sys.stderr)
    print(
        f"  See {_override_doc_pointer()} for override options.",
        file=sys.stderr,
    )
    print(top, file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
